message:
{{#is_alert}}The box egress proxy has rejected its box files for at least 5 minutes and is running on its last good config. Boxes made since then have no egress (their agents can't reach their model providers). Logins, including removals, still apply.

**Check:** the squid pod's `squid-reload: WARN` lines give the file count and how long it has failed. The file at fault is one of the egress-lists generator's writes into `squid-boxes`. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md, "How fast a box gets egress".{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The egress lists generator couldn't read `egress-held` (missing, unreadable, or holding an entry that isn't a box namespace), so it is keeping every box's proxy login. A box whose start Core gave up on may still have web access while this lasts, though it's scaled to 0.

**Check:** the generator's `egress-held ALERT` lines give the error type. The ConfigMap `switch-hosted/egress-held` must exist; only the scaler writes it. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The box remover refused to delete a box listed for removal: its namespace belongs to another machine than the list says, Core doesn't back the removal, or the entry is out of form. Nothing was deleted. A namespace of another machine is the case to look at first: a box may have been listed by mistake.

**Check:** the remover's `Remover ALERT` lines give the namespace and the reason; Core's alerts name boxes it doesn't back. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The box operator refused part of a box removal: a claim out of form, a namespace that belongs to another machine, Core refusing the confirmation, or a request to make a box under a retired name. Nothing was deleted or made.

**Check:** the provisioner's `Box operator ALERT` lines give the namespace and the reason. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The egress lists skipped a box-owners entry: it isn't a box namespace, or its owner isn't a keyed id. That box gets no web access until it's fixed.

**Check:** the egress lists' `box-owners ALERT` lines give the namespace. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A running box uses an image the operator doesn't pin or allow. Someone may have run a box outside the image allow-list.

**Check:** the operator's `Box operator ALERT` line gives the namespace; compare its image with the allowed list. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The operator refused to bind a spare box that already has an owner. That could be an attempt to give one person's box to another.

**Check:** the operator's `Box operator ALERT` lines around the time, and Core's claim records for that spare. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The reaper couldn't delete a spare box, or Core refused it as already gone. The spare keeps its place until it's cleared.

**Check:** the reaper's `Reaper ALERT` lines give the spare and the reason. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A new node has been Ready for longer than the gate allows but still carries its gate taint, so no box can land on it.

**Check:** the node gate's `Node gate ALERT` line names the node; look at its checks. See deploy/hosted/DEPLOY.md.{{/is_alert}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE
